Worked Example 2 (cont.)
Point \(p^1=[1,0,0]^T\) in frame \(\{1\}\):
\[\begin{bmatrix}p^0\\1\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}0&-1&0&1\\1&0&0&2\\0&0&1&0\\0&0&0&1\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}1\\3\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}\]
So \(p^0=[1,3,0]^T\).

Worked Example: Transform a Point


Using \(H_1^0\) from the previous slide, transform \(p^1=[1,\,0.5,\,0]^T\):
\[\begin{bmatrix}p^0\\1\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}0&-1&0&1\\1&0&0&2\\0&0&1&0\\0&0&0&1\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}1\\0.5\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}0.5\\3\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}\]
So \(p^0=[0.5,\,3,\,0]^T\).

Example: Finding All Solutions
def find_all_ik_solutions(robot, T_target, n_attempts=10):
    """Try multiple initial guesses to find different solutions."""
    solutions = []

    for _ in range(n_attempts):
        # Random initial guess
        q0 = np.random.uniform(-np.pi, np.pi, robot.n)

        sol = robot.ikine_LM(T_target, q0=q0)

        if sol.success:
            # Check if this is a new solution
            is_new = True
            for existing in solutions:
                if np.allclose(sol.q, existing, atol=0.1):
                    is_new = False
                    break

            if is_new:
                solutions.append(sol.q)

    return solutions

