The Offset Instruction
FANUC’s built-in offset command
! Move to P[1] with an offset stored in PR[5]
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[5] ;
The robot moves to P[1] shifted by the XYZ values in PR[5].
Example: Approach and retract pattern
! Define approach offset (50mm above)
PR[10,1]=0 ;
PR[10,2]=0 ;
PR[10,3]=50.0 ;

! Approach → Pick → Retract
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[10] ;
L P[1] 100mm/sec FINE ;
CALL HAND_CLOSE ;
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[10] ;

The Offset instruction is FANUC’s cleaner way of doing what we just did manually with PR math. Instead of copying PR[1] to PR[2] and modifying Z, you just tell the motion command “go to P[1] but shifted by the values in PR[10].” The offset is applied in the user frame coordinates, so Z+50 means 50mm up in the user frame, not necessarily in the world frame — this is important if your user frame is tilted. The advantage over manual PR math is that you don’t need a temporary PR to hold the computed position, and the code is more readable. The offset PR only needs XYZ values — the orientation comes from the base position P[1]. This pattern is used extensively in industrial programs: define a few offset PRs at the beginning (approach height, retract height, shift distances) and reuse them throughout the program.

Multi-Program Architecture
Real applications use multiple programs calling each other
/PROG MAIN
   1: UFRAME_NUM=1 ;
   2: UTOOL_NUM=1 ;
   3: LBL[1] ;
   4: CALL PICK ;
   5: CALL PLACE ;
   6: JMP LBL[1] ;
/END

/PROG PICK
   1: J P[1:Pick_App] 100% FINE ;
   2: L P[2:Pick] 100mm/sec FINE ;
   3: CALL HAND_CLOSE ;
   4: L P[1:Pick_App] 500mm/sec FINE ;
/END

/PROG PLACE
   1: J P[1:Place_App] 100% FINE ;
   2: L P[2:Place] 100mm/sec FINE ;
   3: CALL HAND_OPEN ;
   4: L P[1:Place_App] 500mm/sec FINE ;
/END

Structuring code into multiple programs is essential for maintainable industrial applications. The MAIN program sets up the frames and runs the high-level loop. PICK and PLACE are self-contained subroutines with their own local positions (P[]). This structure has several benefits: each program is short and easy to understand; you can test PICK or PLACE independently by running them standalone; if the pick location changes, you only edit the PICK program; and multiple main programs can reuse the same PICK and PLACE subroutines. Notice that MAIN uses CALL, which transfers execution to the subprogram and returns when it finishes — like a function call in Python. The P[] positions are local to each program, so P[1] in PICK is a different position than P[1] in PLACE — no collision. The LBL/JMP pair creates an infinite loop. In a real application, you’d add a condition to exit the loop (part counter, sensor input, or operator stop). Also notice the position labels like P[1:Pick_App] — these comment strings make the code self-documenting.

Simple Palletizing with Offsets
Placing parts in a grid pattern using math
/PROG PALLETIZE
   1: R[1]=0 ;               ! Part counter
   2: LBL[1] ;
   3: CALL PICK ;
   4: ! Calculate column and row
   5: R[2]=R[1] MOD 4 ;      ! Column (0-3)
   6: R[3]=R[1] DIV 4 ;      ! Row (0-2)
   7: ! Build offset
   8: PR[10,1]=R[2]*50.0 ;   ! X = col * 50mm pitch
   9: PR[10,2]=R[3]*50.0 ;   ! Y = row * 50mm pitch
  10: PR[10,3]=0 ;
  11: ! Place with offset
  12: L P[5:Pallet_Origin] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[10] ;
  13: CALL HAND_OPEN ;
  14: R[1]=R[1]+1 ;
  16: IF R[1]&lt;12, JMP LBL[1] ;  ! 4x3 = 12 parts
/END

This is where all the concepts come together: registers, position registers, offsets, and math. The pattern fills a 4-column by 3-row grid (12 positions total) by teaching just one point — the pallet origin (corner). The MOD and DIV operations are the key trick: for part number N, MOD 4 gives the column (0,1,2,3) and DIV 4 gives the row (0,1,2). Multiplying by 50.0 converts to millimeters assuming a 50mm pitch between positions. The Offset instruction adds this computed displacement to the pallet origin. Walk through a few examples: part 0 goes to column 0, row 0 (the origin). Part 1 goes to column 1, row 0 (50mm in X). Part 4 goes to column 0, row 1 (50mm in Y — first part of second row). Part 11, the last one, goes to column 3, row 2 (150mm X, 100mm Y). This approach scales easily — change the MOD/DIV values and the pitch to handle different grid sizes. FANUC also has a built-in palletizing function for more complex patterns, but understanding the math behind it makes you a better programmer.

Exit Ticket: Solution
! Define approach offset
PR[10,1]=0 ; PR[10,2]=0 ; PR[10,3]=40.0 ;
! Define place offset
PR[11,1]=200.0 ; PR[11,2]=0 ; PR[11,3]=0 ;

! Approach pick
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[10] ;
! Pick
L P[1] 100mm/sec FINE ;
CALL HAND_CLOSE ;
! Retract
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[10] ;
! Approach place (combine offsets via PR math)
PR[12]=P[1] ;
PR[12,1]=PR[12,1]+200.0 ;
L PR[12] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[10] ;
! Place
L PR[12] 100mm/sec FINE ;
CALL HAND_OPEN ;
! Retract
L PR[12] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[10] ;

Walk through the solution step by step. The approach offset PR[10] is reused four times — for pick approach, pick retract, place approach, and place retract. This is the power of storing offsets in PRs. The place position PR[12] is computed by copying P[1] and adding 200mm to the X component. Note that we reuse the same approach offset for both pick and place — this ensures consistent approach height everywhere. An alternative solution would use the Offset instruction with two offsets combined, but the PR math approach shown here is more explicit and easier to debug. Point out that the approach and retract speeds are different from the final approach speed — fast in free space, slow near the part. The HAND_CLOSE/HAND_OPEN macros handle the I/O and timing internally — without the wait inside the macro, the robot might start retracting before the gripper has finished closing.

Solution 1: Cylindrical Manipulator — Geometric Result (2/2)
\[
x = d_3\cos\theta_1 = 0.65\cos 60^\circ = 0.3250\text{ m}
\] \[
y = d_3\sin\theta_1 = 0.65\sin 60^\circ = 0.5629\text{ m}
\] \[
z = d_1 + d_2 = 0.50 + 0.35 = 0.8500\text{ m}
\]
The joint variables are cylindrical coordinates: \((\theta_1, z, r) = (\theta_1, d_1+d_2, d_3)\).
Geometric interpretation: \(\theta_1\) sets the azimuth, \(d_2\) sets the height above the base, and \(d_3\) sets the radial reach. No coupling between joints — each controls exactly one cylindrical coordinate.

Subroutines with CALL
Break programs into reusable parts — just like functions in Python:
/PROG MAIN
   1:  CALL PICK ;         ! Run the PICK subprogram
   2:  CALL PLACE ;        ! Run the PLACE subprogram
   3:  JMP LBL[1] ;        ! Loop
/END
/PROG PICK
   1:  L P[1:Approach] 500mm/sec FINE ;
   2:  L P[2:Grasp] 100mm/sec FINE ;
   3:  DO[1:Gripper]=ON ;
   4:  WAIT  .30(sec) ;
   5:  L P[1:Approach] 500mm/sec FINE ;
/END
Use CALL to keep programs short and reusable — just like functions in code.

Position Register Math (Offsets)
PR components: PR[n,1]=X, PR[n,2]=Y, PR[n,3]=Z
Offset a position by 50mm in X:
PR[10]=P[1:Base] ;        ! Copy base position
PR[10,1]=PR[10,1]+50.0 ;  ! Shift X by 50mm
L PR[10] 200mm/sec FINE ;  ! Move to offset position
3×3 grid palletizing pattern:
PR[10]=P[1:Corner] ;        ! Grid origin
FOR R[1]=0 TO 2 ;
  FOR R[2]=0 TO 2 ;
    PR[10,1]=P[1:Corner,1]+R[2]*50 ;  ! X offset
    PR[10,2]=P[1:Corner,2]+R[1]*50 ;  ! Y offset
    L PR[10] 300mm/sec FINE ;
    CALL PLACE ;
  ENDFOR ;
ENDFOR ;

Loop Patterns: WHILE and DO-WHILE
FANUC TP has no native WHILE keyword — build it with LBL/IF/JMP:


WHILE pattern (check before body):
LBL[1] ;
  IF R[1]&gt;=10, JMP LBL[2] ;  ! exit condition
  ! --- loop body ---
  L PR[1] 500mm/sec FINE ;
  R[1]=R[1]+1 ;
  JMP LBL[1] ;                ! loop back
LBL[2] ;
Runs zero or more times.

DO-WHILE pattern (check after body):
LBL[1] ;
  ! --- loop body ---
  WAIT DI[1]=ON ;
  CALL PROCESS_PART ;
  R[1]=R[1]+1 ;
  IF R[1]&lt;10, JMP LBL[1] ;   ! loop back
Runs at least once.

Built-in Offset Instructions
Instead of manually computing PR math, FANUC provides dedicated offset instructions:


General Offset (relative to User Frame):
! Set offset condition
OFFSET CONDITION PR[10] ;
! Move with offset applied
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Offset ;

! Or apply directly:
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Offset,PR[10] ;
Shifts the target point by PR[10] values in the active User Frame directions.

Tool Offset (relative to Tool Frame):
! Set offset condition
TOOL_OFFSET CONDITION PR[20] ;
! Move with tool offset applied
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Tool_Offset ;

! Or apply directly:
L P[1] 500mm/sec FINE Tool_Offset,PR[20] ;
Shifts along the tool axes — useful for approach/retract along the tool Z.

Key difference: General Offset shifts in world/user frame directions. Tool Offset shifts in the tool’s own coordinate system (e.g., approach 50mm above a surface along the gripper axis regardless of orientation).

RRT: Pseudocode
def rrt(start, goal, obstacles, delta=0.5, max_iter=5000, goal_bias=0.05):
    tree = {start: None}           # node -&gt; parent

    for i in range(max_iter):
        if random() &lt; goal_bias:
            q_rand = goal          # occasionally aim at goal
        else:
            q_rand = random_config()  # otherwise explore

        q_near = nearest(tree, q_rand)      # closest tree node
        q_new = steer(q_near, q_rand, delta)  # one step toward sample

        if collision_free(q_near, q_new, obstacles):
            tree[q_new] = q_near   # keep only valid edges

            if (distance(q_new, goal) &lt; delta and
                collision_free(q_new, goal, obstacles)):
                tree[goal] = q_new  # connect final edge
                return reconstruct_path(tree, goal)

    return None                    # iteration budget exhausted

Walk through each function: random_config() returns a uniformly random point in C-space bounds. nearest() finds the closest tree node by Euclidean distance (or C-space metric). steer() moves from q_near toward q_rand by exactly delta distance (or less if q_rand is closer). collision_free() checks if the straight-line segment is obstacle-free (typically by sampling points along the segment). Goal biasing is a crucial practical trick: with probability 5%, we sample the goal itself as q_rand, which occasionally tries to grow the tree directly toward the goal, dramatically speeding up convergence.

RRT*: Additional Steps
def rrt_star(start, goal, obstacles, delta=0.5, max_iter=5000):
    nodes = {start: None}
    cost = {start: 0.0}

    for i in range(max_iter):
        q_rand = sample(goal)
        q_near = nearest(nodes, q_rand)
        q_new = steer(q_near, q_rand, delta)

        if not collision_free(q_near, q_new, obstacles):
            continue

        # --- RRT* additions ---
        r = rewire_radius(len(nodes))
        neighbors = nodes_within(nodes, q_new, r)

        # 1. Choose best parent
        best_parent = q_near
        best_cost = cost[q_near] + dist(q_near, q_new)
        for n in neighbors:
            c = cost[n] + dist(n, q_new)
            if c &lt; best_cost and collision_free(n, q_new, obstacles):
                best_parent = n
                best_cost = c
        nodes[q_new] = best_parent
        cost[q_new] = best_cost

        # 2. Rewire neighbors
        for n in neighbors:
            new_cost = cost[q_new] + dist(q_new, n)
            if new_cost &lt; cost[n] and collision_free(q_new, n, obstacles):
                nodes[n] = q_new
                cost[n] = new_cost

The two new blocks are clearly marked. The choose-best-parent step is like looking around the neighborhood when you arrive somewhere new and picking the best route to get there. The rewire step is like telling your neighbors “hey, it might be faster to go through me” — if your new position creates a shortcut, the tree restructures itself. Both operations require collision checks, making RRT* roughly 2-3× slower per iteration than basic RRT. But the path quality improvement is dramatic and worth it in most applications.

Homogeneous Form: One Matrix Does Both
The same operation as a single matrix multiply (the 2D version of W02’s \(T_{WS}\)):
\[
\begin{bmatrix} x^W_i \\ y^W_i \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
= \underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta & -\sin\theta & x_R \\ \sin\theta & \cos\theta & y_R \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}}_{T_{WS}(x_R,\, y_R,\, \theta)}
\begin{bmatrix} x^S_i \\ y^S_i \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
\]






Tip


Why bother with the \(3 \times 3\) form? Rotation and translation become one operation, and transforms compose by matrix multiplication: \(T_{WS} = T_{WR}\, T_{RS}\) if the LiDAR is offset from the robot center. Identical trick to the \(4 \times 4\) homogeneous transforms in W02, just one dimension smaller.




The 3x3 form is not strictly necessary for our lab – the rotate-then-translate form is easier to code in NumPy. But it’s the right concept to show because it makes the connection to W01-W02 explicit. The extra row [0, 0, 1] and the extra coordinate “1” are bookkeeping that lets matrix multiplication reproduce “rotate then translate” in one shot. The payoff is composition: if the LiDAR is mounted 10 cm in front of the robot center, you can express that as T_RS (robot-to-sensor, a constant) and then T_WS = T_WR * T_RS. This is exactly what we did in W02-W03 for manipulator frames – the math is the same, the frames are different.
